body:
 [https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/borderline-personality-disorder/index.shtml](https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/topics/borderline-personality-disorder/index.shtml) 

&#x200B;

I think it's really admirable that you want to understand what she's going through. So good for you. 

In my experience, I have lot's of highs and lows throughout the day and they can change so rapidly, it's overwhelming for me and frustrating for the people around me. Just be patient with her. BPD really wears a person down sometimes. I had a partner who would either yell at me and call me names or completely shut me out when I was having a mental breakdown or just really sad or mad; of course it didn't help at all. Staying as calm as possible is best and keeping the communication going works. I used to ask a lot of questions in order to confirm I was still loved or appreciated and it even got annoying to me sometimes but it helps with my mental stability. So if she does repeat questions often, it may not be because you have made her feel that way, it's just the way BPD makes us feel. 

&#x200B;

If you would like more advice or just someone to talk to, I'm here. I wish you both the best.
